MT-A (Lean-ready, restricted; a literature result restated for SAT). For CIMS input with M clauses, FK-DPLL makes at most M^O(log^2 M) calls. It needs four elementary lemmas:
(a) restriction + UP + subsumption preserves CIMS and never increases |P| or |N|;
(b) sum of 2^-|C| < 1 implies SAT, with a constructive witness;
(c) the frequency lemma: a clause of width <= log2 M that meets every opposite clause contains a variable in a 1/log2 M fraction of them;
(d) the recurrence A(v) <= 1 + A(v-1) + A((1-eps)v).
None is machine-checked, and my transcription of Algorithm A is from memory.

MT-B (Lean-ready). Model-set equality for the miss-weakening N' of section C.

MT-C (the real gap; open, do not assume). CORE-TAU: a branching rule that is not given the core and still makes at most (M+tau(F))^polylog(M) calls on arbitrary CNF F, where tau is the minimum number of minimal literal-transversals over unsatisfiable subformulas. Any version with tau replaced by a polynomial in M for all CNF gives quasi-polynomial SAT and contradicts ETH, so the target must keep tau or another honest parameter. This sharpens round 94's WORK-MONOTONE: it holds for class-preserving extras and for extras of bounded miss-degree (bound (M*w^d)^O(log^2)), and is open otherwise.

Separately open in the literature since 1996: whether MT-A's exponent can be made constant (monotone duality in P). Nothing here addresses it.
